\section{Choosing a route}{

Select peaks by retention time using \code{\link{get_peaks}} when the samples are
aligned, or can be aligned with \code{\link{correct_rt}}, and no other peak falls
within \code{tol} of a target. Only the peaks near the targets are fitted, which
is quicker, and each target can be measured at its own wavelength.

Match peaks to a reference using \code{\link{match_peaks}} when retention times alone
cannot identify the targets (e.g., in crowded windows). Peaks are matched by
spectrum as well as retention time, so a target can be picked out from its
neighbors, and the retention-time window can be wider to allow for drift.
Every peak is fitted, and every target is measured at the same wavelength.
}

\section{Constructing the targets table}{

Build a \code{data.frame} with an \code{rt} column giving the retention time of each target
and, optionally:
\itemize{
\item \code{name}: Unique names, used as peak table column names.
\item \code{lambda}: The wavelength at which to measure each target. When matching to
a reference, it must equal the \code{lambda} given to \code{\link{match_peaks}}.
\item \code{tol}: How far each target's peak may lie from its \code{rt}, in minutes,
regardless of the time unit of the chromatograms. Defaults to \code{0.1}.
\item \code{spectral_cutoff}: Used only by \code{\link{match_peaks}}. The minimum spectral
correlation for a match to each target's reference peak. Defaults to the
\code{spectral_cutoff} argument of \code{\link{match_peaks}}.
\item \code{rt_window}: Used only by \code{\link{match_peaks}}. The maximum difference in
retention time, in minutes, between each target's reference peak and its
match in another sample. Defaults to the \code{rt_window} argument of
\code{\link{match_peaks}}.
}
}

\section{Selecting peaks by retention time}{

\enumerate{
\item Call \code{get_peaks(chrom_list, targets = targets)}. In each sample, \code{get_peaks}
fits only the peaks whose apex lies within \code{tol} of a target.
\item Call \code{\link{get_peaktable}} on the result. The peak table has one column per
target found in at least one sample; a target found in no sample gets no
column.
}

Peaks are assigned by retention time only, so each target must stay within
\code{tol} of its listed time in every sample, and closer to it than to any
neighboring target. If the samples drift more than that, fit warping models
with \code{\link{correct_rt}} and pass them as a \code{mod_list}; the targets are then found
on the aligned time axis.

When more than one peak falls within \code{tol} of a target, the \code{select_by}
argument of \code{\link{get_peaks}} decides which is retained. The default,
\code{select_by = "height"}, keeps the tallest, which may not be the target;
narrow \code{tol}, or set \code{select_by = "proximity"} to keep the peak closest to
the target. Peaks are selected before they are fitted, so \code{"height"}
compares the signal at each apex, including any baseline beneath it.

To keep every candidate in the peak list, set \code{select_by = "none"}. Where a
sample has several peaks for one target, \code{\link{get_peaktable}} enters the one
with the largest \code{response} in the table, but summarizes the target's
\code{pk_meta} over all of them, so its \code{rt} can fall between the candidates. To
take the metadata from the most intense peak instead, set
\code{summarize_by = "max"} in \code{\link{get_peaktable}}.
}

\section{Matching peaks to a reference sample}{

\enumerate{
\item Call \code{\link{get_peaks}} without providing \code{targets}, so that every peak is fitted.
\item Call \code{match_peaks(peak_list, targets = targets, lambda = ...)}.
Unless \code{reference} is supplied, \code{match_peaks} picks as reference a sample that
has a peak for every target, then matches each sample's peaks to the
reference peaks by spectral correlation and retention time.
\item Call \code{get_peaktable(matched, pks = peak_list)}. Unlike the other
pathway, the peak table will still include untargeted peaks. Use
\verb{[, targets$name]} to keep only the targets.
}

In the reference sample, each target is assigned to the tallest well-fitted
peak within \code{tol} minutes of the specified \code{rt}. A peak in another sample can
match a reference peak only if it lies within \code{rt_window} minutes of it and
their spectral correlation is above the \code{spectral_cutoff}.
